# --- F-8 backup retention (HANDOFF 3g) --------------------------------------
# Nightly archives carry the full account database and every hub secret. Before
# this, NOTHING pruned them: not the local copies, not the rclone destination,
# not the provider's trash or version history. Every retention window in the
# published privacy policy was defeated by that, because the last copy of a
# deleted record lived forever in an archive nobody ever removed.
#
# 30 days for full sensitive backups AND for Litestream recovery history.
# 90 days is the published ceiling and is enforced as a hard cap, not a
# suggestion -- a value above it aborts the deploy rather than quietly
# publishing a false retention claim.
#
# Pruning must reach ALL FOUR copies. Missing any one leaves the claim false:
#   1. local archives under /var/backups/winsvpn
#   2. objects at the rclone destination
#   3. the provider's trash / version history behind that destination
#   4. provider-side lifecycle copies
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS_DEFAULT="${BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS:-30}"
BACKUP_RETENTION_CEILING_DAYS_DEFAULT="${BACKUP_RETENTION_CEILING_DAYS:-90}"
# Sanitised schema and deployment manifests are NOT sensitive backups: no
# accounts, no device metadata, no secrets. They are retained separately and
# longer so a restore has something to rebuild against after the sensitive
# window has closed.
BACKUP_MANIFEST_RETENTION_DAYS_DEFAULT="${BACKUP_MANIFEST_RETENTION_DAYS:-365}"
# Set to 1 to make the pruner report what it would remove and remove nothing.
BACKUP_PRUNE_DRY_RUN_DEFAULT="${BACKUP_PRUNE_DRY_RUN:-0}"
# Pruning is part of the retention control, not an optional extra. When the
# pruner cannot prove it reached the remote, the backup run fails.
REQUIRE_BACKUP_PRUNING_DEFAULT="${REQUIRE_BACKUP_PRUNING:-1}"
# --- F-8 independent backup encryption (see write_backup_script) ------------
# The rclone crypt remote encrypts client-side, but it is ONE control whose
# password lives in /root/.config/rclone/rclone.conf ON THIS HOST, and it
# protects nothing at all on the local fallback path (/var/backups/winsvpn),
# which is written whenever the remote is unavailable. age with RECIPIENT
# PUBLIC KEYS is the second, independent control: the host holds only public
# halves, so a seized or rooted hub cannot read its own backups.
#
# Required by default, and the requirement is checked at PREFLIGHT rather than
# at the first backup run -- an unsatisfiable gate that fires after every host
# mutation, immediately before public cutover, is worse than no gate at all.
# The recipients file carries no secret: it is public keys only, and the
# decision it represents is where the matching identities are escrowed.
BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS_FILE_DEFAULT="${BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS_FILE:-/etc/winsvpn/backup-recipients.age}"
REQUIRE_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_DEFAULT="${REQUIRE_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION:-1}"
# Backup destination identity. `hostname -s` is NOT unique across this fleet:
# the Kansas hub and the Spain edge both report "ubuntu", so two hosts sharing
# one rclone remote would write into the same winsvpn-backups/<host> prefix and
# overwrite and prune each other's archives. resolve_backup_host_id mints a
# stable, fleet-unique value once per host and pins it in
# /etc/winsvpn/backup.env so the backup and prune scripts always agree.
BACKUP_HOST_ID_DEFAULT="${BACKUP_HOST_ID:-}"
BACKUP_HOST_ID_FILE_DEFAULT="${BACKUP_HOST_ID_FILE:-/etc/winsvpn/backup-host-id}"
