# Per-server LOAD indicator (lane L85, Round LD; meaning revised by Round LD2).
# GET /nodes gives every listed server `load_pct`: an int 0..100 in steps of 5,
# or null when unknown. The bar means CONGESTION: how many people are using the
# server and how busy it is, not its line speed (owner ruling 2026-09-29). The
# hub publishes only three coarse bands, never a fine-grained figure and never
# a user count: the node reports ratios, the hub stores only the band.
# WHAT THE BAND DOES REVEAL (owner ruling: it is a head-count signal):
#   * The node's capacity is a planning policy (90 x min(vCPUs, 3), floored at
#     40), not a measurement. From a CONFIRMED low band, busy needs 50 % of it
#     (45, 90 or 135 users; 20 at the floor) for two reports in a row.
#   * With NO confirmed band (a cold start, or after two or more unknown
#     reports in a row), the plain boundary applies and busy starts at 40 %:
#     36, 72 or 108 users (16 at the floor of 40).
#   * Someone who controls many devices can add active devices until the band
#     flips and so estimate how many other people are on that node (the edge
#     minus their own devices, to within the two-report confirmation). The
#     bands make that coarse and slow (about four minutes per probe); they do
#     not prevent it.
#   * The capacity floor is applied last, after any max_peers cap, so no
#     configuration turns a small head count into a band edge.
#   * Double VPN traffic is not in the exit's head count: the core's single link
#     peer is excluded and its riders never appear at the exit as peers, so it
#     shows there only through the CPU term.
# The listed status ("congested") is published separately and does not move the
# bar (Round LD OI-6b: an immediate override let the bar jump on one report).
# Unknown (null): the node never reported, its last usable load report is
# older than NODE_LOAD_REPORT_STALE_SECONDS, the report lacked the CPU or the
# active-users ratio, no band has been confirmed yet, or the listed node is
# not online/congested and reachable. A gateway restart or any one missed
# sampler makes a report unknown and blanks the bar until the next usable
# report, but the confirmed band (never published while unknown) is HELD for
# NODE_LOAD_HOLD_SECONDS so that one unknown report does not reset hysteresis.
# The stale window is a client contract value, so it is deliberately not
# tunable from the environment.
NODE_LOAD_REPORT_STALE_SECONDS = 180
# One unknown report (about 120 s) between two usable ones is 240 s to 248 s
# apart at the measured cadence; two unknown reports in a row are 360 s apart.
NODE_LOAD_HOLD_SECONDS = 300
NODE_LOAD_BAND_PCT = {"low": 25, "busy": 60, "full": 90}
# Band boundaries on raw congestion = max(CPU ratio, active-users ratio[, WAN
# ratio, only when the node's operator configured a link capacity]).
# A band is left only when raw pressure is NODE_LOAD_HYSTERESIS_PCT beyond the
# boundary, and a new band is taken only after NODE_LOAD_CONFIRM_REPORTS
# consecutive reports point at or beyond it. From "low", busy therefore starts at 50 and
# full at 85; from "full", busy returns below 65 and low below 30.
NODE_LOAD_BOUNDARY_BUSY_PCT = 40.0
NODE_LOAD_BOUNDARY_FULL_PCT = 75.0
NODE_LOAD_HYSTERESIS_PCT = 10.0
NODE_LOAD_CONFIRM_REPORTS = 2
